.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 6, 2026
.Dt WAIT_READABLE 2
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm wait_readable
.Nd wait for readable Pipe or Console handles
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In absd/syscall.h
.Ft int64_t
.Fn absd_wait_readable "const uint64_t *handles" "uint64_t count" "uint64_t ticks"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel copies one through eleven full handle values from readable user
memory and returns a positional readiness mask.
Bit zero corresponds to the first handle; duplicate handles have separate bits.
Every member must name a current Pipe or Console and hold READ rights.
The entire set is validated before any readiness is returned.
.Pp
A Pipe is ready when it contains bytes or has no writers, including empty EOF.
A Console is ready when input is buffered.
Readiness consumes no bytes and does not promise that a subsequent read will
succeed or avoid blocking: another reader may consume the input first.
.Pp
Zero ticks waits indefinitely.
.Dv ABSD_WAIT_READABLE_POLL
.Pq UINT64_MAX
polls immediately and returns zero if no member is ready.
Other values expire at the checked deadline
.Va now + ticks + 1
in delivered timer ticks and return error \-110.
The first serialized completion wins; readiness does not take precedence
over a simultaneous timeout.
This is not a wall-clock deadline.
.Pp
All completions revalidate the original full handle and object generations.
A closed or revoked member refuses the whole set even if another is ready.
Stopping or retiring a task cancels its wait state and cannot revive it.
There is no persistent subscription, callback, or write-readiness interface.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Returns a nonnegative positional mask or a negative kernel error.
The C wrapper returns the raw signed result.
Rust bindings
.Fn absd_user::wait_readable
and
.Fn absd_runtime::wait_readable
accept a slice of u64 handles and return a Result containing the mask or error.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "-110"
.It \-1
A member lacks READ rights.
.It \-9
A member is invalid, stale, or not a Pipe or Console.
.It \-14
The handle array is not readable user memory.
.It \-22
Count is zero or greater than eleven, or the finite deadline overflows.
.It \-38
The kernel does not implement this call.
.It \-110
The finite wait expired.
.El
.Sh ABI
This is syscall 0x7fa8, additive to kernel ABI generation 1.
On x86-64, use int 0x80 with RAX holding the number, RDI the array address,
RSI the count, RDX the ticks, and RAX receiving the signed result.
On AArch64, use svc 0 with x8 holding the number, x0 the array address,
x1 the count, x2 the ticks, and x0 receiving the signed result.
.Pp
.Dv ABSD_WAIT_READABLE_MAX
is eleven.
The wrappers always issue the call, including an empty set, so an older
ABI 1 kernel consistently returns \-38.
Existing ABI 1 programs retain their previous calls.
FSD serving startup refuses an unsupported kernel with
.Ql FSD FAILED kernel lacks wait_readable
and exits 3 before writable startup; it does not fall back to polling.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr handles 7
